WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:06.040
"Star Trek: Picard" meets "Deep Space Nine"
in the first episode of season 2 – and that's

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:10.470
not the only Easter egg buried in this reference-heavy
show.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:15.280
The premiere episode of "Picard" Season 2
opens with Starfleet officers scurrying through

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:18.960
the corridors in the middle of what appears
to be a battle aboard a starship.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.089
Arriving on the bridge, things aren't looking
good, as an unseen villain is tapping into

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:26.239
the ship's systems and appears to be gaining
control.

00:00:26.239 --> 00:00:31.150
Unable to overpower the enemy, Picard commands
the ship's computer to activate the auto-destruct

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:32.150
sequence.

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:37.840
We later learn the enemy is the Borg, and
longtime fans will note this isn't the first

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.600
time that Picard has had to order self-destruct
due to a Borg invasion.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:46.850
It is, however, a major change from his previous
behavior, and shows he's learned a lesson

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:49.950
from a certain former Klingon security chief.

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:54.690
In the 1996 film "Star Trek: First Contact,"
the Borg had infiltrated the Enterprise and

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.079
had controlled more than half the ship.

00:00:57.079 --> 00:01:01.760
Picard's trusted officer and friend, Lieutenant
Commander Worf, recommended they self-destruct

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.470
the ship to prevent its total takeover.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:09.909
Picard adamantly refused to destroy it, and
it wasn't until he got a lecture from Lily

00:01:09.909 --> 00:01:13.350
that he relented and set the ship to self-destruct.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:18.090
This time, it seems, Picard is much quicker
to respond, having learned that when the Borg

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:24.539
take over, sometimes it's best to activate
the ship's auto-destruct system right away.

00:01:24.539 --> 00:01:28.999
As many eagle eyed viewers noticed in the
first teaser for the new season, a major piece

00:01:28.999 --> 00:01:33.509
of "Deep Space Nine" lore appeared prominently
on the table in Picard's library.

00:01:33.509 --> 00:01:38.259
It appeared that the former Enterprise captain
-- an avid archeologist -- was studying the

00:01:38.259 --> 00:01:43.660
Reckoning Tablet, which originally made its
first and only appearance in "Star Trek: Deep

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:44.660
Space Nine."

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:47.850
"I see you recognize it."

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:52.740
In the episode "The Reckoning," the tablet
was smashed to pieces by Captain Sisko after

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.130
he received a vision.

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:59.389
In doing so, he released the villainous Pah-wraiths
that were locked in a centuries-long war with

00:01:59.389 --> 00:02:00.860
the Bajoran Prophets.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:05.670
Many wondered if the appearance of the tablet
was an Easter egg just for trailers, perhaps

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.490
as a simple reference to "Star Trek: Deep
Space Nine."

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:12.560
But the tablet does indeed appear in the opening
episode of "Picard" season two.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.420
As Picard and Laris speak in his library,
it appears on the table between them in the

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:19.830
center of the screen, and it is still in pieces.

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:24.500
There's a viewing glass above it, which suggests
Picard has indeed been studying it.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:29.160
Given its prominence in the scene, we wonder
if it's more than just an Easter egg, and

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.030
perhaps a hint of more to come.

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:36.561
As the first episode transitions to Picard's
speech at Starfleet Academy, we see Picard

00:02:36.561 --> 00:02:42.030
-- in his new role as Chancellor of the Academy
-- standing before a group of incoming Cadets.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:47.120
Behind him is a series of flags, presumably
of Federation member worlds.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.510
That's notable because there's a number of
flags from worlds who weren't members of the

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.720
Federation during "The Next Generation."

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:58.320
While we do see flags for the Tellarites and
Vulcans, who were founding members, we see

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.480
a Klingon flag as well.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.950
Longtime allies of the Federation since at
least the days of "TNG," it seems they could

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.490
now be full members of the UFP.

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:12.360
We also see the Bajoran flag, indicating that
Bajor may have finally gained membership following

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.360
"Deep Space Nine."

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.970
But the most curious flag is the Ferengi [fuh-REN-ghee]
flag.

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:21.320
Though Ferenginar was on its way to change,
with the appointment of the less greed-focused

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:26.140
Rom as their Grand Nagus at the end of "Deep
Space Nine," it's curious that they'd become

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:27.810
Federation members so quickly.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:32.970
"A new Ferenginar needs a new kind of Nagus.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.630
A kinder, gentler Nagus.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:36.430
And that's you my boy!"

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:41.450
If these aren't the flags of Federation members,
then it's possible these are simply the flags

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:45.880
of worlds from which Starfleet Academy has
found cadets -- meaning the Ferengi flag is

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:50.070
a reference to the character Nog, the young
Ferengi who attended the academy and served

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:53.470
aboard the Defiant on "Deep Space Nine."

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:58.870
During his speech, Picard is proud to introduce
his friend Elnor as the first "fully Romulan"

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:00.760
entrant into Starfleet Academy.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:05.150
This indicates that, at some point prior to
the events of the episode, the Academy had

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:08.930
admitted other cadets with at least partial
Romulan ancestry.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:13.860
It's notable also because there has been at
least one Starfleet officer that we've seen

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:19.510
whose bloodline could be traced to Romulans:
A crewman named Simon Tarses, from "The Next

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:21.350
Generation" episode "The Drumhead."

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:26.040
Tarses was investigated for his possible role
in sabotaging the Enterprise, but his real

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:31.350
crime was lying about his parentage by claiming
he was part Vulcan rather than Romulan.

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:35.180
"Isn't it true that the paternal grandfather
of whom you speak was not a Vulcan but was

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:37.120
in fact a Romulan?

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.060
That it is Romulan blood you carry?"

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:43.920
Ultimately, Tarses did not even attend the
Academy, and chose to enlist instead.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.270
Nevertheless, it was heavily implied that
there was great controversy surrounding officers

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.530
who carried the blood of a Federation enemy.

00:04:50.530 --> 00:04:55.070
It remains to be seen if this will be an issue
for Elnor, who vocalizes his concerns about

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:59.190
fitting in at the Academy due to his race,
though Picard's gift -- a book written by

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:01.540
Spock -- should help.

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:06.900
The "Picard" Season 2 premiere gives us a
surprising glimpse into the childhood of Jean-Luc

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:10.720
Picard, and hints that something terrible
may have happened to his mother when he was

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:11.720
young.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:15.830
But it's his mother's inspiring words to the
young Picard, as he looked up into the night

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:19.020
sky, that are the source of character drama
in this episode.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:23.940
In flashbacks, we see how Yvette Picard nurtured
her son's love of the stars, offering to build

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.960
him an observatory of sorts on the family
estate.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:32.000
We know from the "TNG" episode "Family" that
Picard's father and brother both decried his

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.889
choice to enter Starfleet, so it seems a fitting
revelation that it was his mother who helped

00:05:36.889 --> 00:05:39.390
ignite his exploratory spirit.

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:44.610
But in his speech to the Academy, Picard chooses
to quote his mother with a phrase that clearly

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:47.980
has stuck with him all of his life: "let's
see what's out there."

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:53.060
It's an inspiring moment, and one that affected
Picard greatly, and diehard Trekkies will

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:58.280
remember this line for its appearance in the
"TNG" premiere episode "Encounter At Farpoint."

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:02.590
"Let's see what's out there.

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:03.590
Engage."

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:08.000
Back at the Academy, Cadets await their first
assignments, with an officer calling out names

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.949
and ships for them to report to.

00:06:09.949 --> 00:06:15.160
One Cadet is sent to the Hikaru Sulu, clearly
named for the helmsman of the original USS

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:17.720
Enterprise, originally played by George Takei.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:22.490
In "Star Trek Generations," we met Sulu's
daughter, who helmed the Enterprise-B -- and

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:26.690
it's clear from this new starship that her
father's name has carried a powerful legacy

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:28.900
even into the 25th century.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:33.350
Other ships include the Excelsior -- captained
by Raffi -- where Elnor is assigned, and the

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:34.460
USS Grissom.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:39.780
The original Excelsior was captained by Sulu
himself in "Star Trek VI: The Undiscovered

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:40.780
Country."

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:46.200
While it wouldn't be unprecedented if Elnor's
ship were the same Excelsior, it's most likely

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.540
a newer ship with the same name.

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:53.270
The Grissom meanwhile, though not seen, was
the name of the science vessel that carried

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:58.780
Lt. Saavik and David Marcus to the Genesis
Planet in "Star Trek III: The Search For Spock."

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:03.210
Admiral Picard also mentions to Raffi his
intention to make some modifications to the

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:07.919
Kobayashi Maru test, which is designed to
evaluate an officer's decision-making skills

00:07:07.919 --> 00:07:09.860
in a no-win situation.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:15.720
This test has been seen or referenced several
times, most famously in "Star Trek II: The

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:18.919
Wrath Of Khan," but again in JJ Abrams' "Star
Trek" reboot.

00:07:18.919 --> 00:07:19.919
Guinan's bar

00:07:19.919 --> 00:07:24.240
When Picard visits his old friend Guinan,
who is now tending a bar in Los Angeles, he

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:29.550
goes to an address that will make fans of
"The Next Generation" smile: 10 Forward Street.

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:33.419
Fans will recall the location of Guinan's
bar on the Enterprise as Ten Forward, but

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:35.270
that's hardly the only Easter egg.

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:39.050
Behind the bar, we see bottles of Cardassian
Kanar from "Deep Space Nine".

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:43.401
Also on the wall is a sign for Arcanis Lager,
first seen in "Star Trek III: The Search for

00:07:43.401 --> 00:07:44.401
Spock."

00:07:44.401 --> 00:07:49.110
When the two sit down for a drink she offers
up some options but selects a bottle of Saurian

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:53.790
Brandy, a classic "Trek" ale seen many times
throughout the franchise.

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:58.400
Here she refers to it as "hooch," seeming
to reveal the drink as something less than

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.479
a classy top shelf spirit.

00:08:00.479 --> 00:08:06.640
When they first meet, Picard notes that Guinan's
people, the El-Aurians age slowly, and she

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:11.630
tells him that aging is something that happens
"only if we choose to" -- and that she has

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:16.580
chosen to for the benefit of humans who dislike
being reminded of their own mortality.

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:21.790
This helps explain to audiences why the centuries-old
Guinan, played by Whoopi Goldberg, looks 20

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:25.180
years older than the last time we saw her
in "Star Trek: Nemesis."

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:29.280
She then makes a few clever nods to classic
"TNG" episodes, referencing the times that

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.380
they stood side-by-side to face a new galactic
threat, like they did with the Borg in the

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:34.669
episode "Q Who."

00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:38.849
She also references the events of "Yesterday's
Enterprise" when she recalls the time that

00:08:38.849 --> 00:08:40.190
they jumped universes together."

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:44.880
But Picard hasn't come to reminisce, and is
instead looking for some advice regarding

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:45.980
his love life.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:50.890
Picard insists that he has loved before, and
quite deeply, to which Guinan responds,

00:08:50.890 --> 00:08:53.930
"But always to those who would only be temporary."

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:58.620
It's true that Picard has had a handful of
dalliances in "Trek," and the most obvious

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:03.250
candidate is renegade archeologist Vash whom
he met in "Captain's Holiday" -- who Picard

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:07.120
clearly held a deep and passionate attraction
towards.

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:11.930
But as Guinan said, Vash was never a good
match because she was always on the move,

00:09:11.930 --> 00:09:14.730
and Picard's first love was Starfleet.

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:18.970
Their most haunting exchange is when she tells
Picard that he should know better than most

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:21.410
that there are worse things than being dead.

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:26.210
This is a clear reference to his previous
assimilation by the Borg, when they stole

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:31.130
his body and mind and used him as an instrument
of destruction.

